Theorem 5.2 says that for suitably typed terms \((\lambda x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1}.\,\tau)(\sigma_0,\ldots,\sigma_{n-1}) = \tau[\sigma_0/x_0,\ldots,\sigma_{n-1}/x_{n-1}]\), proved for \(n = 1\) by induction on \(\tau\): a constant gives \((\lambda x.\,k)(\sigma) = k\), a variable gives \((\lambda x.\,x)(\sigma) = \sigma\), a tuple distributes as \(\langle(\lambda x.\,\tau_0)(\sigma), (\lambda x.\,\tau_1)(\sigma)\rangle\) and then uses the inductive hypothesis, an application rewrites the left side as \(\mathrm{eval}\) of \((\lambda x.\,\langle\tau_0,\tau_1\rangle)(\sigma)\), and an abstraction satisfies \((\lambda x.\,\lambda y.\,\tau)(\sigma) = \lambda y.\,\tau[\sigma/x]\) when \(y\) is not free in \(\sigma\), using an argument swap and the rule that \(\alpha(y) = \beta\) with \(y\) not free in \(\alpha\) yields \(\alpha = \lambda y.\,\beta\). `beta` states the one-variable semantic equation that evaluation of the curry of an arbitrary approximable \(g\) paired with the constant map of \(\sigma\) equals \(g\) composed with the pairing of the identity and that same constant map, proved by extensionality and the composition, pairing, evaluation, curry, constant, and identity laws. `beta_tuple` states that the curry of a pairing, evaluated at a context and at \(x\), is the pair of the two curried components. `beta_abs` states that the double curry evaluated at \(v\), then \(\sigma\), then \(y\), equals \(g\) at the pair of the pair of \(v\) and \(\sigma\) with \(y\), which is \(h(v)(\sigma)(y) = g(v,\sigma,y)\) and not the freeness side condition or the argument swap. `subst_value` repeats the single curry law and is not the constant or variable base equation. `theorem_52` is the conjunction of these four. **Theorem:** partial — the one-variable map equation and the tuple and double-curry identities are proved, and the syntactic \(n\)-ary equation, the base equations as stated, the application case, and the abstraction rule with the freeness proviso are not. **Proof:** partial — the argument is extensional rewriting by curry, evaluation, and pairing, not the induction on term complexity.
